  "narrative": "In the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles have become an integral part of their identity and cultural expression. The natural hair movement has gained significant momentum, with many embracing their textured locks in their unmanipulated state, sans chemical relaxers or heat-straightening. Hairstyles such as afros, created by combing out natural growth, and cornrows, a traditional African braiding practice, have become increasingly popular. Protective hairstyles like box braids and dreadlocks (also known as locs) are also favored for their ability to minimize hair breakage and promote healthy hair growth. These styles not only reflect the women's cultural heritage but also serve as a means of self-expression and empowerment. As African-American culture continues to evolve, these hairstyles have become an integral part of its identity, with many women proudly donning them as a symbol of their roots and resilience.",
